sections.s2_technical:
SQL databases store data in tables with a fixed schema defined up front, and they enforce relationships through foreign keys. They provide ACID transactions, so a group of changes either all commit or all roll back, which is essential when correctness is non-negotiable. They support powerful queries with joins, aggregations, and ad-hoc filtering, which is invaluable for reporting and reconciliation. The cost is that scaling writes beyond one machine is hard and requires sharding, and the rigid schema makes rapid shape changes awkward.

```java
// SQL: a transactional, relational operation with a join-friendly model
@Transactional
public void transferWithAudit(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    jdbc.update("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ?", amount, from);
    jdbc.update("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?", amount, to);
    jdbc.update("INSERT INTO audit(from_id,to_id,amount) VALUES (?,?,?)", from, to, amount);
}
```

NoSQL covers several shapes for different needs. Document stores like MongoDB hold flexible JSON-like documents, good when records vary or evolve quickly. Key-value stores like DynamoDB and Redis give extremely fast access by key. Wide-column stores like Cassandra scale writes massively across nodes. Graph stores like Neo4j model richly connected data like fraud networks. The common theme is designing the data model around the queries you will run, denormalizing so a read is a single lookup rather than a join.

```java
// NoSQL (document): a self-contained, denormalized record read in one lookup
customerDoc.put("_id", "SAV2001");
customerDoc.put("profile", Map.of("name","Rajesh","tier","gold"));
customerDoc.put("recentLogins", List.of("2026-11-01T02:00Z", "2026-10-31T21:00Z"));
// no joins needed: everything for this customer is in one document
```

The decision criteria are concrete. Choose SQL when you need strict ACID transactions, when data is highly relational with many joins, when the schema is well-defined and stable, and when you need flexible ad-hoc queries, which describes a bank ledger. Choose NoSQL when you need to scale horizontally to very high volume, when the schema varies or evolves fast, and when access is predominantly by a known key, which describes activity logs and session stores. A senior nuance is that this is not either-or; modern systems use polyglot persistence, SQL for the transactional core and NoSQL for scale-out workloads, and some databases now blur the line with NewSQL offering ACID at scale, so I justify each choice by the specific workload rather than dogma.

